               DIGITAL RAISES THE PERSONAL WORKSTATION
             PRICE/PERFORMANCE STANDARD WITH CELEBRIS XL
                   AND INTEL PENTIUM PRO PROCESSOR


            New High-Performance, Windows NT-Based System
       with Processor-Independent Architecture Ensures Smooth
      Transition, and Provides Long-Term Investment Protection


MAYNARD, Mass., November 1, 1995 -- Digital Equipment Corporation 
today announced that the industry's fastest personal workstation 
family now includes the Celebris XL 6150.  In addition, the company 
also announced the PC industry's fastest, easiest upgrade to Intel's 
32-bit Pentium Pro processor - via a simple daughtercard swap - the 
only upgrade approach of its kind.  Digital also will incorporate 
the Pentium Pro processor in its award-winning line of Prioris PC 
servers in early 1996.  Digital is also demonstrating the Celebris 
XL system with 200MHz Pentium Pro processor technology at a press 
conference held by Intel in San Francisco today.

     The addition of Intel's Pentium Pro processor brings explosive 
performance to the Intel version of Digital's personal workstation 
family, as part of Digital's strategy to keep its personal 
workstation family, introduced last month, on the leading edge of 
processing performance.  Combined with Windows NT, Digital's 
personal workstation family provides users of technical and 
compute-intensive applications a combination of workstation-class 
performance and shrink-wrapped applications, with the look, feel 
and pricing of a PC.

     Digital's personal workstation family includes models based on 
both Intel and Digital Alpha processors, and features components and 
options that are interchangeable between the two platforms.  The 
processor-independent architecture enables fast, smooth and easy 
migration to the latest Intel Pentium, Pentium Pro and Digital Alpha 
microprocessors by simply plugging in the respective daughtercards.  
This unique approach positions Digital's personal workstation family 
as the Windows NT platform of choice for technical users, offering 
the highest level of price/performance for mission-critical 
computing.

     "Digital's popular Celebris XL line is acclaimed by technical 
and power users," said Howard Elias, vice president, Desktop and 
Mobile Product Lines for Digital's Personal Computer Business Unit.  
"Now, without skipping a beat, our customers can enjoy a fast, easy 
migration to Pentium Pro within a proven, reliable system - that is 
built, distributed and supported by a company they know and trust."

     "Digital is moving quickly to set new performance levels for 
workstations," said Carl Everett, senior vice president of the 
Desktop Products Group, Intel Corporation.  "The Pentium Pro 
processor based Celebris XL personal workstation enables users to 
take advantage of the increased performance that the Pentium Pro 
processor provides to 32-bit environments."

Personal Workstation Solutions Across the Enterprise

     The Digital personal workstation family is ideal for users of 
compute- and graphics-intensive design applications as well as 
software engineering, desktop publishing and financial services.  As 
one of the top workstation vendors, Digital understands what it 
takes to compete and win in the workstation market. The company has 
worked with all of the major Independent Software Vendors (ISVs) for 
more than a decade.  With more than 2,500 native Windows NT applications 
available on Intel-based systems and more than 1,500 available on Alpha, 
Digital is uniquely positioned to provide a flexible, powerful platform 
to meet the exploding Windows NT market.  

Digital's personal workstation family delivers:

     o 	Superior performance at far lower price points than most 
        competing UNIX-based workstations;
 
     o 	The new Celebris XL 150MHz Pentium Pro processor-based 
        model, added to the existing seven Pentium and Alpha models 
        introduced in September 1995;

     o 	The first workstation released by Digital to incorporate 
        ClientWORKS, a client management software package that 
        includes extensions of Digital's leading Desktop Management 
        Interface (DMI) implementation and additional client 
        management capabilities;
 
     o 	The broadest range of Windows NT workstation performance on 
        a common platform with a dual architecture and 
        interchangeable components, built and backed by a solid, 
        industry-leading manufacturer;
 
     o 	Easy daughtercard upgrades to higher performance processors 
        as customers' business needs change, for long-term 
        investment protection, via Digital's PowerGrade program;
 
     o 	New high-end 3D graphics adapter from AccelGraphics for 
        workstation-class performance;

     o 	Optimized Windows NT application performance via Digital's 
        relationships with leading technical applications suppliers, 
        including Autodesk, Inc., Bentley Systems Inc., and 
        Parametric Technology Corporation.

Selling Strategy Provides Winning Value Proposition

     The Celebris XL personal workstations are available through 
Digital's resellers, distributors and Value Added Resellers (VARs).  
The personal workstation family's common componentry and options 
benefit resellers by simplifying inventory management and product 
training.  The company's channel strategy is aimed at the technical 
market's premier VARs that target specific technical applications, 
such as those from Autodesk, Bentley, and Parametric.

     "The Celebris XL personal workstation is ideally suited for the 
technical application users we target," said Nader Khoshniyati, 
Chief Executive Officer, microCAD Technologies, Inc. "With Digital's 
extensive Windows NT service and support offerings, coupled with its 
superb hardware and networking technology, we are selling a product 
that's in the right place at the right time. What more could a 
vendor ask for?"

Entry Level and Packaged Systems Available

     Digital's new Celebris XL 6150 Pentium Pro model and PowerGrade 
upgrade (for existing Celebris XL and DECpc XL systems) are 
available now, with volume deliveries in December.  The new Celebris 
XL 6150 personal workstation is PCI-based, with Windows NT 
factory-installed, and is available in three configurations.  

All configurations, including entry-level, include:

     o 	Minimum 16MB RAM, 256KB packaged cache, a 1.44MB floppy 
        disk, keyboard and three-button mouse priced at $4,599;

     o 	The Midrange configuration also includes a 4X SCSI CD ROM, 
        Matrox Millennium 3D Graphics Adapter, 1.0 GB SCSI hard disk 
        and Windows NT factory installed, priced at $5,549;

     o 	The High-end configuration also includes 32MB RAM total, a 
        4X SCSI CD ROM, AccelGraphics AG300 3D Graphics Adapter, 
        2.0GB Wide SCSI Drive, an Adaptec Ultra SCSI Adapter and 
        Windows NT factory installed priced at $9,849.

     o 	The 150MHz PowerGrade for existing Celebris XL users is 
        priced at $1,999.

Enterprise-Level Commitment to State-of-the-Art Processors

     Digital will feature the new Celebris XL personal workstation 
at AutoFact in Chicago Nov. 14 - 16 and at COMDEX in Las Vegas Nov. 
13 - 17.  Also at COMDEX, Digital will feature an advanced 
technology demonstration of future Prioris Pentium Pro servers.  
Digital is targeting enterprise-wide applications performance with 
its Prioris family of Pentium Pro-based, fault-tolerant Symmetrical 
Multi-Processing (SMP) server systems.  The new Prioris servers with 
Pentium Pro processors will provide the increased scalability and 
performance that users demand to satisfy their enterprise computing 
requirements.

Backed By Industry's Best Hardware Warranty, Software Support

     Digital provides the industry's leading warranty for its new 
personal workstations, including a comprehensive three-year hardware 
warranty with one-year on-site support.  Also, all personal 
workstations include 90 days of advisory telephone support for the 
Microsoft Windows NT operating system, as well as for application or 
factory-installed software purchased from Digital and included on 
Digital's User Application Support product listing.

     Digital and Microsoft have a long-standing relationship.  As a 
result of the Digital/Microsoft strategic "Alliance for Enterprise 
Computing," Digital is training an additional 1,500 engineers to 
complement its existing 800 Microsoft-certified engineers.  Digital 
provides a full spectrum of Windows NT-related service and support 
for both stand-alone and networked environments, including 
consulting, telephone support, training and application development 
support.

     Digital Equipment Corporation is the world's leader in open 
client/server solutions from personal computers to integrated 
worldwide information systems.  Digital's scaleable Intel and Alpha 
platforms, storage, networking, software and services, together with 
industry-focused solutions from business partners, help 
organizations compete and win in today's global marketplace.
