         DIGITAL UNVEILS SOFTWARE TRANSLATION BREAKTHROUGH,
         ENABLES HIGH-PERFORMANCE X86 COMPATIBILITY ON ALPHA


       ...No. 1 RISC Architecture for Windows Applications...


MAYNARD, Mass., November 6, 1995 -- Today, Digital Equipment 
Corporation moved dramatically forward toward achieving its goal of 
complete x86 application compatibility on Alpha systems with the 
introduction of an advanced software translation and emulation 
technology, FX!32.

     "Our vision is to provide Alpha users with the same suite of 
Windows applications that run on x86 systems," said Ed Caldwell, 
vice president, Digital Semiconductor, a Digital Equipment 
Corporation business.  "Transparent access to 32-bit Windows 
applications will be accomplished via FX!32 for 32-bit native x86 
applications, as well as through our broad portfolio of native 
Windows NT applications.  All 16-bit applications will be accessible 
through Insignia Solutions' SoftWindows product.

     "The result will be that in 1996 Alpha system users will be 
able to access all their Windows applications as if they were using 
an x86 system.  For Win32 applications, performance will be 
comparable or better than on x86 systems.

Unique Approach

     "Our approach to Win32 application compatibility is unique in 
the industry," Caldwell added.  "By combining innovative binary 
translation and proven emulation techniques, we are able to achieve 
high-performance application compatibility totally through software.  
By contrast, on-chip solutions increase chip real estate, design 
complexity, design time and chip cost, and also adversely impact 
performance."

     Alpha has all the elements needed for success:  unbeatable 
performance, more native Windows NT applications that any other 
vendor except Intel, price/performance leadership systems, a close 
alliance with Microsoft, and now high-performance x86 compatibility.

Win32 Applications Build Market Momentum

     Both the Windows 95 and Windows NT operating systems use the 
Win32 API (Applications Programming Interface).  With the increasing 
market acceptance of these two operating systems, industry watchers 
anticipate a rapid shift in new applications development as well as 
migration of existing 16-bit applications to the Win32 API.  

     FX!32 technology will dramatically change the playing field for 
the acceptance of the Alpha architecture in the Windows market.  
Currently, Alpha is the unsurpassed RISC solution for Win32 
applications, with more than 1,200 native Windows NT applications 
shipping for Alpha systems today, and more than 1,000 additional 
applications committed.  The only other architecture with 
substantial application support on Windows NT is x86.  Once an 
application is ported to Win32, it can be easily recompiled to run 
on Alpha under the Windows NT system. 

x86 Compatibility

     Combining software emulation methods with advanced binary 
translation technology, Digital's FX!32 technology will take 
advantage of Alpha's architectural features, native Alpha runtime 
libraries, and operating system primitives to enable native x86 
32-bit applications to run with high performance on Alpha.

     Andrew Allison, editor/publisher of Inside the New Computer 
Industry, said, "With better-than-native Intel performance of Win32 
applications and Alpha performance on the same processor, this 
product opens up some very interesting opportunities.  Next year is 
shaping up to be a good one for Alpha."

Leading Expertise in Translation Technology

     According to Maurice Marks, Digital Semiconductor's technical 
director, Digital possesses the computing industry's leading 
software translation expertise.  "To date, we have developed five 
binary translators for platform migration.  Among these are the 
highly successful product to migrate VAX applications to Alpha, and 
our Freeport Express translator that enables Alpha systems to run 
SunOS applications at native Alpha speed."

     FX!32 technology is currently being tested;  full release will 
take place in mid-1996. FX!32 will be demonstrated at Fall COMDEX 
95.   

     Digital Semiconductor, a Digital Equipment Corporation business 
headquartered in Hudson, Massachusetts, designs, manufactures and 
markets industry-leading semiconductor products including Alpha 
microprocessors and PCI chips for networking, bridging, and 
graphics/multimedia, as well as low-power StrongARM microprocessors 
under license from Advanced RISC Machines Ltd. Digital Semiconductor 
operates design centers in Hudson;  Palo Alto, California;  Austin, 
