              DIGITAL EXTENDS LEAD IN HIGH-PERFORMANCE
         DATABASE ARENA WITH VERY LARGE MEMORY (VLM) PROGRAM


     ...Establishes VLM64 Program Office; names VP of Enterprise
  Marketing; opens database technology centers; signs new customers
                     for 64-bit Alpha systems...


MAYNARD, Mass., -- November 9, 1995 -- Digital Equipment Corporation 
today launched an aggressive marketing and sales campaign to extend 
its leadership position in the emerging market for Very Large 
Memory, 64-bit database applications. As part of its new VLM64 
campaign, Digital is providing customers and business partners the 
resources, including technical assistance, they need to take 
advantage of VLM64 opportunities. 

     Digital is the only company delivering high-performance 64-bit 
servers, AlphaServer systems, and a 64-bit operating system, Digital 
UNIX, which together make VLM possible.  Digital's VLM64 technology 
dramatically improves database and application performance by 
raising the amount of information that can be stored in memory. All 
32-bit systems are limited to 2 Gbytes of memory. AlphaServer 8400 
systems can store up to 14 Gbytes of data in memory.

     "Our AlphaServer 8000 family has enjoyed tremendous market 
success," said Mike Gallup, Digital vice president of worldwide 
systems marketing. "In fact, in just six months, we have shipped 
more AlphaServer 8000s than the total number of IBM SP2s shipped in 
the first two years of that product's life. And major customers have 
replaced other vendors' products with AlphaServer 8000 systems."

     "VLM64 means breakthrough performance for data warehousing, 
on-line transaction processing, decision support, business and 
financial applications, and other line-of-business applications," 
said Gallup. "Getting the highest levels of database performance is 
critical to ensuring maximum business success, whether it be in 
health care, telecommunications or manufacturing. VLM64 is the 
solution. And only Digital offers it now."

Digital's VLM64 effort already includes the following: 

     o 	Formation of the VLM64 Program Office, with worldwide 
        responsibility for leading all VLM64 marketing and business 
        partner programs.  The VLM64 Program Office is headed by 
        Jerry Vennard, vice president of Enterprise Solutions 
        Marketing.

     o	VLM64 support by key database vendors -- Informix, Oracle, 
        and Sybase, with Software AG in the future. These database 
        applications will realize dramatic improvements in 
        performance and affordability. End-user applications running 
        VLM64 databases also will gain significant performance 
        gains.

     o	Acceptance by a broad range of customers, such as Baxter 
        Healthcare, the American Society of Civil Engineers, and 
        many others.

     o	The establishment of Customer Expertise Centers and joint 
        engineering centers in cooperation with key business 
        partners -- with the common goal of accelerating the growth 
        of VLM64 applications and their implementation.

     o  A sales "strike force" formed to create and take advantage 
        of VLM64 business opportunities.

     "The rapid emergence of VLM64 database capabilities presents a 
great opportunity for Digital and its business partners, and an even 
larger opportunity for customers seeking a competitive advantage 
from information technology," said Vennard. "There is widespread 
industry agreement that 64-bit computing is the enabling technology 
for VLM. Before other vendors get 64-bit systems to market, many of 
our customers already will be seeing returns on their VLM64 
investments."

     The new programs and incentives will make it fast and effective 
for IT managers, business users, and Digital business partners to 
profitably leverage the breakthrough performance and affordability 
of Alpha technology.

     "Analysts note that Digital is at least 18 months ahead of our 
competitors in this market," Vennard added. "Already, millions of 
people rely on Alpha-based computers every day.  And no one comes 
close to Digital in the super-critical high-end market, where the 
AlphaServer 8000 family is in a class of its own."

     "Digital's AlphaServer 8000 has rapidly matured to become a 
consistent leader in high-end commercial performance and price-
performance," said Peter Kastner, vice president at the Aberdeen 
Group, a Boston-based computer industry market research and 
consulting firm. "The affordable power shown in the latest tpc-C 
tests translates into tangible benefits for today's savvy server 
buyers and their applications." 

     The AlphaServer 8400 holds the industry record for the tpc-C 
open systems benchmark, with 9414 tpmC, running the Oracle7 database 
on Digital UNIX, and price-performance of $316 per tpmC. 
         
Growing Industry Interest in VLM64 

     The need for improved customer service, faster time-to-market, 
improved product quality, and more cost-effective management of 
global operations is fueling customer acceptance of the VLM64 
database option. Responding to these needs, companies are generating 
and maintaining more information electronically, inflating the size 
of their databases, and carrying higher applications loads. 

     Digital and its partners are helping companies use VLM64 
technology to move growing data volumes closer to business 
functions, where they can be accessed quickly for customer service, 
trend analysis, instant interaction, and other strategic purposes. 

Customers are Taking Advantage of VLM64

     Customer in a variety of industries, such as health care, 
retailing, telecommunications, and banking, view their early 
adoption of Digital's VLM64 solutions as a key competitive 
advantage. 

     For example, Baxter Healthcare replaced its IBM mainframes with 
AlphaServer 8400s running SAP R/3 and the Oracle7 database.

     The American Society of Civil Engineers replaced its Unisys 
mainframe with an AlphaServer 8000 system, Digital UNIX and Oracle7.

VLM64 Program Office 

     o	Works with the application development groups of database 
        partners to provide customers the expertise and support 
        needed to begin building competitive VLM-based information 
        solutions immediately. 

     o	Contributes experience in applying 64-bit computing in VLM 
        systems. 

     o  Oversees a global network of expertise centers, for 
        developing applications in finance, industrial, 
        communications and cross-industry applications. 

     o 	Works with Digital's database partners to foster development 
        of applications leveraging the benefits of VLM64 solutions.  

     Digital's AlphaServer 8400 and 8200 symmetic multiprocessing 
RISC systems employ the world's fastest microprocessor, the 300MHz 
Alpha 21164 microprocessor. VLM64 capabilities are available on 
systems using the true full 64-bit Digital UNIX operating system.
The systems run Informix, Oracle, and Sybase databases with the 
VLM64 option. The systems also run the OpenVMS operating system.  

     Digital Equipment Corporation is the world's leader in open 
client/server solutions from personal computing to integrated 
worldwide information systems.  Digital's scalable Alpha platforms, 
storage, networking, software and services, together with industry-
focused solutions from business partners, help organizations compete 
and win in today's global marketplace. 
