             DIGITAL SEMICONDUCTOR, CUSTOMERS SHOW ALPHA
          PERFORMANCE, WINDOWS COMPATIBILITY AT FALL COMDEX


             ...Graphics, Bridge, Network Chips Shine...


LAS VEGAS, November 13, 1995 -- Alpha leadership performance with 
full X86 compatibility, desktop video applications, and PCI 
connectivity products for the open market headline Digital 
Semiconductor's exhibit at COMDEX Fall '95, today through Friday 
at the Las Vegas Convention Center.

     At Booth 4018 in the North Hall, Digital Semiconductor 
highlights systems using its Alpha microprocessors, using Digital's 
new FX!32 technology for high-performance x86 compatibility to run 
mainstream Windows applications.  The exhibit also features 
Digital's PCI peripheral chips and systems from 14 Alpha customers, 
running an array of imaging, animation, and computer-aided design 
(CAD) software.

     Digital's FX!32 emulation/translation software for Alpha, 
announced last week, is being demonstrated publicly for the first 
time on the show floor and in booth theater presentations.

Market Momentum Growing

     "Digital Semiconductor is rapidly building momentum as a 
merchant chip vendor," said William N. Johnson, vice president, 
Semiconductor Marketing and Sales.  "Alpha is emerging as the number 
one RISC architecture for the Windows market.  It is already the 
runaway performance leader for native-mode applications, and our new 
FX!32 technology for x86 compatibility will put Alpha ahead of the 
pack for all 32-bit Windows applications.

     "Add to that our market-leading PCI networking and bridge 
chips, our growing family of video accelerators, and low-power 
StrongARM microprocessors due out next year, and you have a picture 
of a dynamic, growing merchant business," Johnson said.

Alpha Takes on the Competition

     The Digital Semiconductor exhibit features performance 
comparisons of systems based on the Alpha 21164-333MHz, Pentium 
133MHz, PowerPC 604, and MIPS R4400 microprocessors.  Systems 
demonstrate popular Windows NT applications for computer-aided 
design, animation, and imaging, such as MicroStation, AutoCAD, 
LightWave, Elastic Reality, and Pro/Engineer, and Animation Master 
software.  Times are recorded for each run and announced to 
determine the top performers.

     To demonstrate the performance of Digital's FX!32 translation-
emulation technology for x86 compatibility on Alpha, Alpha 21064A 
and Pentium 90 PCs run identical, mainstream Windows applications 
for productivity and development, including Microsoft Office, Adobe 
Photoshop, Lotus Notes, and Visual Basic software.

PCI Graphics, Bridge Chips Support PCs

     Digital Semiconductor is also displaying its portfolio of PCI 
peripheral chips.  The 21130 video/graphics accelerator chip 
supports full-motion video on Pentium PCs running Windows 95 and 
Windows 3.1 software, while the 21052 PCI-to-PCI bridge chip is 
shown linking PCI buses in a fully configured, clear-panel Digital 
Prioris HX server.
Alpha customers demonstrating products in the Digital Semiconductor 
booth include:

